{"source_slots":{"s0":"My first thought is making sure the soil doesn’t dry out completely while I’m away—especially since the afternoon heat pulls moisture so fast. I’m thinking about setting up a simple drip system with a bottle, but I don’t want to spend much on gear. Maybe just using what I already have?","s2":"Oh, the bottle trick with the tiny hole—that’s clever. I’ve seen that before but never tried it. I do have a couple of big yogurt tubs, so I might just go with the sacrificial container since it’s easier to refill. Less risk of clogging than poking holes in a bottle cap. And yeah, mulch would help, but I don’t have any on hand right now. Anyway, thanks—this gives me a couple of options that won’t cost me anything.","s3":"You're welcome! It sounds like a very low-cost, high-reward approach. 

Since you're looking for something free, you're in luck with your yogurt tubs—they can definitely double as the watering containers for your setup.

If you're not using mulch, remember that even a simple layer of dried leaves or grass clippings placed on top of the soil can act as an improvised, free mulch layer to help retain that moisture and prevent the surface from baking in the afternoon heat!"}}
